@startuml
title EmberWatch | Ingestion, alerting, and history/live merge
skinparam backgroundColor #F8FAFC
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam ArrowColor #475569
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

actor "Virtual gateway" as Gateway
participant "Express /devices" as Ingest
participant "Ingestor + live state" as State
participant "Anomaly engine" as Anomaly
participant "Bounded batch writer" as Writer
database "MongoDB" as DB
participant "Socket.IO /live" as Socket
actor "Authorized dashboard" as Client
participant "History API" as History

== Telemetry ingestion ==
Gateway -> Ingest : Authenticated batch\n(sid, seq, ts, metrics)
Ingest -> State : Validate, rate-limit,\ncheck source and sequence
alt invalid, duplicate, or overloaded
  State --> Ingest : Reject / duplicate / retryAfterMs
  Ingest --> Gateway : ACK with counts and backoff
else accepted event
  State -> State : Update latest state only\nfor newer sequence
  State -> Writer : Enqueue reading
  State -> Anomaly : Evaluate EWMA, thresholds,\ndwell and fresh neighbours
  opt incident transition
    Anomaly -> DB : Persist / update incident
    Anomaly -> Socket : Reliable scoped alert
    Socket --> Client : Alert for authorized zone
  end
  Ingest --> Gateway : Queued-acceptance ACK
end

group Periodic persistence and live delivery
  Writer -> DB : insertMany batch\n(about every 500 ms)
  DB --> Writer : Write result
  State -> Socket : Changed-source deltas\n(about every 250 ms)
  Socket --> Client : Volatile, room-scoped frame
end

== Historical view joined to live updates ==
Client -> Socket : Subscribe to authorized zone/source
Socket --> Client : Snapshot + active incidents
Client -> History : Request time range
History -> DB : Read raw events / minute rollups\nwith sequence cursor
DB --> History : Persisted history
History --> Client : Points + lastSeq watermark
Client -> Client : Merge buffered live points\nby sequence; cap and downsample
Socket --> Client : Continue live deltas

note over Gateway,DB
  The ACK confirms bounded in-memory queue admission,
  not durable commit. A process crash can lose queued data.
  MongoDB's unique (sid, seq) index is the persistence
  dedupe boundary; volatile telemetry may be coalesced.
end note
@enduml
